            <div xml:id="d353">

                <pb facs="0474" n="406" sameAs="frus:frus1867p1/pg406"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>Mr. de Stoeckl to Mr. Seward</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New York</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1867-08-20"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >August</hi> 20, 1867</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Secretary of State:</hi> In reply to the letter
                    you have done me the honor to address to me on the 15th of this month, I make it
                    a duty to inform <pb facs="0475" n="407" xml:id="pg407"/> you that the only
                    point at which we had a military garrison was at Sitka. The number of men we
                    maintained there never exceeded two hundred, and latterly there were at Sitka
                    only 80 men. The barracks in which these troops are stationed, and which will be
                    turned over to the federal government, may conveniently hold from a hundred to a
                    hundred and twenty men. Besides the garrison at Sitka, there are some advanced
                    posts on the mainland, occupied by four or fi<hi rendition="#italic">ve</hi> men
                    each. These posts are situated far to the north, and it will be difficult to
                    turn them over to the United States troops before next spring. As to what
                    relates to provisions, the federal government will be able to supply itself in
                    the warehouses of the Russo-American Company with breadstuffs, butter, sugar,
                    tea, and salt fish. In the Sitka market may be found fresh fish, goat meat,
                    wild, in small quantities. It would be well for the American government to take
                    measures for provisioning its troops with fresh meat or salted, coffee, and
                    other articles which are used in the United States army.</p>
                <p>Please accept, Mr. Secretary of State, the assurance of my very high
                    consideration.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>STOECKL.</signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
